  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-4,7).$",
  "worked_solution": "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,7),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-4-3)^{2} & =4p(7) \\\\ 49 & =28p \\\\ p & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{7}{4})y \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
